   "title": "Jonathan Vatsa, 200 Others Join SDP in Niger",
   "url": "https://www.thisdaylive.com/2026/10/31/jonathan-vatsa-200-others-join-sdp-in-niger/",
   "domain": "thisdaylive.com",
   "source": "ThisDay（尼日利亚）",
   "published": "Sat, 31 Oct 2026 02:15:53 +0000",
   "_dt": 1793412953.0,
   "ageDays": 0.04,
   "summary": "After quitting the All Progressives Congress (APC), former Niger State Commissioner for Information, Mr. Jonathan Tsado Vatsa and 200 former supporters of the party have joined the Social Democratic Party (SDP). The former APC chieftain and his supporters declared for the SDP at Anguwa-Gulu Vatsa Ward in the Lapai Local Government Area of Niger State on Wednesday.",
   "summary_zh": "退出进步大会党（APC）后，尼日利亚尼杰尔州前信息委员Jonathan Tsado Vatsa与该党200名前支持者加入了社会民主党（SDP）。这位前APC党魁及其支持者于周三在尼杰尔州Lapai地方政府辖区的Anguwa-Gulu Vatsa选区宣布支持SDP。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "After quitting the All Progressives Congress (APC), former Niger State Commissioner for Information, Mr. Jonathan Tsado Vatsa and 200 former supporters of the party have joined the Social Democratic Party (SDP). The former APC chieftain and his supporters declared for the SDP at Anguwa-Gulu Vatsa Ward in the Lapai Local Government Area of Niger State on Wednesday. He was issued with SDP membership card N10120100001 by the ward Chairman of the party, Alhaji Adamu Mohammed Baba. Vatsas’ supporters at the brief ceremony were also issued with SDP membership cards after they submitted their former APC cards. Chairman of the party while presenting the membership card to the man he described as a “big fish” maintained that his contributions to the development of the area remained unequalled. “The decision you have taken today to leave APC is long overdue, it is a welcome development because the party has failed Nigerians,” Baba said. With your (Vatsa) grassroots political experience and your contributions to the political development of the state, we are confident that SDP will take over the reins of power in Niger State in 2027,” he added. Founded on January 22, 1995, THISDAY is published by THISDAY NEWSPAPERS LTD., 35 Creek Road Apapa, Lagos, Nigeria with offices in 36 states of Nigeria , the Federal Capital Territory and around the world. It is Nigeria’s most authoritative news media available on all platforms for the political, business, professional and diplomatic elite and broader middle classes while serving as the meeting point of new ideas, culture and technology for the aspirationals and millennials. The newspaper is a public trust dedicated to the pursuit of truth and reason covering a range of issues from breaking news to politics, business, the markets, the arts, sports and community to the crossroads of people and society. You can email us at: hello@thisdaylive.com or visit our contact us page",
