   "title": "Illegal Lithium Mining: EFCC Arrests 14 Chinese, One Nigerian in Ogun",
   "url": "https://businesspost.ng/general/illegal-lithium-mining-efcc-arrests-14-chinese-one-nigerian-in-ogun",
   "domain": "businesspost.ng",
   "source": "businesspost.ng",
   "published": "Mon, 26 Oct 2026 14:56:23 GMT",
   "_dt": 1793026583.0,
   "ageDays": 4.51,
   "summary": "The Economic and Financial Crimes Commission (EFCC) has arrested 15 suspects, comprising 14 Chinese and one Nigerian, at four illegal lithium processing plants in the Ogere area of Ogun State. The commission disclosed this in a post on its official X account, stating that operatives of its Lagos Zonal Directorate 2 carried out the arrests on Friday, October 23, 2026, following credible intelligence and surveillance.",
   "summary_zh": "经济和金融犯罪委员会（EFCC）在奥贡州奥格雷地区4家非法锂加工厂逮捕了15名嫌疑人，其中包括14名中国人和1名尼日利亚人。该委员会在官方X账号发布的一则帖文中透露，拉各斯第2分局执法人员于2026年10月23日星期五根据可靠情报和 surveillance 实施了逮捕。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "The Economic and Financial Crimes Commission (EFCC) has arrested 15 suspects, comprising 14 Chinese and one Nigerian, at four illegal lithium processing plants in the Ogere area of Ogun State. The commission disclosed this in a post on its official X account, stating that operatives of its Lagos Zonal Directorate 2 carried out the arrests on Friday, October 23, 2026, following credible intelligence and surveillance. Items recovered during the operation included bags of lithium rocks, 18 mobile devices, four computer laptops, two decoders, a television remote control and a trailer truck. The EFCC said the recovered items had been taken as part of its investigation into the activities of the facilities, adding that the suspects would be charged to court upon the conclusion of investigations. However, the agency did not disclose the specific offences the suspects were alleged to have committed or the ownership of the processing plants. Lithium has become a highly sought-after mineral globally because of its critical role in manufacturing rechargeable batteries used in electric vehicles, smartphones, laptops and renewable energy storage systems. Growing demand for electric vehicles and clean energy technologies has intensified the race to secure lithium supplies, making the mineral increasingly valuable and creating incentives for illegal mining and processing. Nigeria is emerging as a new source of lithium in Africa as the world’s largest producers, like Australia and Chile, are unable to fulfil the growing demand worldwide. However, illegal activities thrive in Nigeria’s extractive sector, denying the government due revenues of up to $9 billion, according to the Nigeria Extractive Industries Transparency Initiative (NEITI). The country’s lithium deposits have attracted growing interest from local and foreign operators, while concerns over unregulated extraction, revenue losses, environmental degradation, and the potential circumvention of mining regulations have heightened calls for stricter enforcement. Adedapo Adesanya is a journalist, polymath, and connoisseur of everything art. When he is not writing, he has his nose buried in one of the many books or articles he has bookmarked or simply listening to good music with a bottle of beer or wine. He supports the greatest club in the world, Manchester United F.C",
   "bodyLevel": "fulltext",
   "country": [
    "ng"
   ],
   "topic": [
    "min",
    "ene"
   ],
   "tier": 1,
   "actor": "中国",
